@@ -79,6 +79,33 @@ When you omit `api_backend`, Grok uses `chat_completions`.
 
 To send provider-specific authentication or version headers -- for example, Anthropic's `x-api-key` -- use the `extra_headers` field described below. Grok sends those headers verbatim with every request to the endpoint.
 
+### Strict OpenAI Compatibility
+
+OpenAI-compatible gateways vary in how much provider-specific request data they
+accept. For an endpoint that validates the OpenAI schema strictly, set the
+compatibility policy on that model:
+
+```toml
+[model.gateway-gpt]
+model = "gpt-5"
+base_url = "https://gateway.example/v1"
+api_backend = "responses"
+openai_compat = "strict"
+```
+
+`openai_compat = "strict"` is supported with `chat_completions` and
+`responses`. It suppresses Grok/xAI-only request headers and extensions while
+retaining standard OpenAI fields. In Chat Completions it also uses
+`max_completion_tokens`, removes Grok assistant metadata, and moves image blocks
+returned by client tools out of `role = "tool"` into a following user
+multimodal message. Consecutive tool results remain contiguous so parallel tool
+call ordering is preserved.
+
+The default is `openai_compat = "native"`, which keeps the existing Grok/xAI
+wire behavior. Do not combine `strict` with `api_backend = "messages"`:
+`messages` is the Anthropic Messages protocol, not an OpenAI protocol, and the
+client rejects that combination before sending a request.
+
